WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.570
Submit Puzzle Ideas helps you make your own
puzzle for NINETIMESNINE,

2
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.543
step by step, and send it to the team.

3
00:00:08.893 --> 00:00:12.067
This video shows every entry, what it is for,

4
00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:15.785
and how it fits into the puzzle the wizard
builds.

5
00:00:16.835 --> 00:00:23.790
On thelogicgrid.com, choose Submit Puzzle
Ideas, under One account for every game.

6
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.811
A box asks which puzzle you would like to
make. Choose NINETIMESNINE.

7
00:00:30.861 --> 00:00:34.462
The wizard goes one step at a time. There are
5 steps,

8
00:00:34.482 --> 00:00:37.113
and you can jump back to any of them.

9
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:41.943
Everything you type is saved on this device as
you go.

10
00:00:41.963 --> 00:00:45.843
Leave and come back later, and your draft is
waiting.

11
00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:49.031
Start over clears it when you want a fresh
start.

12
00:00:50.081 --> 00:00:55.031
Watch how it works opens this video and a
written guide to every entry.

13
00:00:55.381 --> 00:00:58.474
Show help on every field shows what to write,

14
00:00:58.494 --> 00:01:02.379
an example, and how each entry is used in your
puzzle.

15
00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.243
Step 1: Your story.

16
00:01:05.593 --> 00:01:08.964
Every NINETIMESNINE puzzle tells a short case:

17
00:01:08.984 --> 00:01:12.378
why the grid needs filling, and how it ends.

18
00:01:13.428 --> 00:01:16.949
Title, which is required. The name of your
puzzle.

19
00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:20.233
Printed at the top of the puzzle and the PDF,

20
00:01:20.253 --> 00:01:23.422
and shown in Saved puzzles and in your credit.

21
00:01:24.472 --> 00:01:27.054
Look. The look that suits your story best.

22
00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:31.757
Picks the pictures and colors when your puzzle
is played in the game.

23
00:01:32.807 --> 00:01:38.887
Opening, which is required. One or two
sentences: who needs the grid filled, and why.

24
00:01:39.237 --> 00:01:44.145
Shown above the grid and printed on the puzzle
page, before the rules.

25
00:01:45.195 --> 00:01:49.355
What is at stake. One short sentence that
makes it matter.

26
00:01:49.705 --> 00:01:53.973
Added after the opening, so players know why
they are solving.

27
00:01:55.023 --> 00:01:59.226
Ending, which is required. What happens when
the grid is full.

28
00:01:59.576 --> 00:02:03.715
Shown when the puzzle is solved, and printed
on the answer key.

29
00:02:04.765 --> 00:02:06.345
Step 2: The grid.

30
00:02:06.695 --> 00:02:11.901
Choose the size and rules of your grid, and
who places the starting numbers.

31
00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:21.101
Grid size, which is required. 4 × 4 and 6 × 6
are quick; 9 × 9 is the classic size.

32
00:02:21.451 --> 00:02:27.553
Every row, column and box holds the numbers 1
to the size, each exactly once.

33
00:02:28.603 --> 00:02:34.086
Diagonals too. Both long diagonals must also
hold every number once.

34
00:02:34.436 --> 00:02:39.407
Adds an extra rule, which also means fewer
starting numbers are needed.

35
00:02:40.457 --> 00:02:44.522
Who places the starting numbers?. Design the
grid yourself,

36
00:02:44.542 --> 00:02:48.223
or let the wizard make one at the level you
choose.

37
00:02:48.573 --> 00:02:53.059
Your numbers are checked: the puzzle must have
exactly one answer,

38
00:02:53.079 --> 00:02:55.209
reachable by reasoning alone.

39
00:02:56.259 --> 00:02:58.307
Step 3: Starting numbers.

40
00:02:58.657 --> 00:03:03.159
Type the numbers players start with. Leave the
other cells empty.

41
00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:08.915
Starting numbers. Type a number in each cell
that should start filled.

42
00:03:08.935 --> 00:03:10.503
Leave the rest empty.

43
00:03:10.853 --> 00:03:13.723
These are the numbers printed in the grid.

44
00:03:13.743 --> 00:03:18.022
The wizard checks there is exactly one way to
fill the rest.

45
00:03:19.072 --> 00:03:21.355
Step 4: Pictures and sounds.

46
00:03:21.705 --> 00:03:25.653
Optional: add your own pictures and sounds for
your puzzle.

47
00:03:26.703 --> 00:03:31.883
Pictures and sounds. Up to 3 pictures and 2
sounds that you made yourself.

48
00:03:31.903 --> 00:03:37.605
For each one, say what it is for, whether we
may use it, and how to credit you.

49
00:03:37.955 --> 00:03:41.416
The team can use them with your puzzle in the
game:

50
00:03:41.436 --> 00:03:45.955
a cover picture, a sound when it is solved, or
background music.

51
00:03:47.005 --> 00:03:51.423
At the bottom of every step, you see your
storyline as you write it:

52
00:03:51.443 --> 00:03:54.921
the puzzle's words, just as players will read
them.

53
00:03:55.271 --> 00:03:59.879
You cannot type in it. It changes as you fill
in the fields above.

54
00:04:00.929 --> 00:04:03.405
Step 5: Credit and permission.

55
00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:08.299
How you would like to be credited, and your
permission to use your puzzle.

56
00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:13.787
Author credit. The name shown in the author
credits of your puzzle.

57
00:04:14.137 --> 00:04:18.767
Printed on your puzzle and shown in the game's
credits if it is published.

58
00:04:19.817 --> 00:04:24.975
Permission, which is required. Tick the box to
say you made the puzzle yourself,

59
00:04:24.995 --> 00:04:29.482
and that the team may use and publish it with
the credit you chose.

60
00:04:29.832 --> 00:04:33.779
We can only save and publish your puzzle with
your permission.

61
00:04:34.829 --> 00:04:37.219
Check my puzzle opens a new page.

62
00:04:37.569 --> 00:04:40.981
The box at the top says whether your puzzle is
ready:

63
00:04:41.001 --> 00:04:45.037
it must have exactly one answer that players
can reason out.

64
00:04:45.387 --> 00:04:47.788
Anything missing or wrong is listed,

65
00:04:47.808 --> 00:04:52.598
with a link straight to the field, and
suggestions to make it better.

66
00:04:53.648 --> 00:04:58.747
Every entry is here in one editor, so you can
fix or change anything.

67
00:04:59.097 --> 00:05:01.753
Each change is checked again straight away,

68
00:05:01.773 --> 00:05:05.221
so you always know whether the puzzle is still
valid.

69
00:05:06.271 --> 00:05:09.173
When you are happy, press Build my puzzle.

70
00:05:09.523 --> 00:05:11.987
Download the PDF to keep or share it,

71
00:05:12.007 --> 00:05:16.521
or print it straight away. It has your puzzle
and its answer key.

72
00:05:16.871 --> 00:05:22.277
Your puzzle is also saved to your Saved
puzzles, so you can play it in the game,

73
00:05:22.297 --> 00:05:26.855
and a copy goes to the team, who may publish
it with your credit.

74
00:05:27.905 --> 00:05:32.333
Want to change something? Make changes takes
you back to the editor,

75
00:05:32.353 --> 00:05:37.015
with suggestions shown and the one-answer
check, then build it again.

76
00:05:38.065 --> 00:05:41.362
Answer the questions, check and fix, then
build,

77
00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:44.295
print and play. Have fun making puzzles!

